    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The change is complete and the test suite is green (2 tests, 0 failures, `BUILD SUCCESS`).\n\n## What I changed\n\nDELETE `/api/owners/{id}` now soft-deletes: it flags the owner and keeps the row, and the create endpoint's duplicate/identity checks skip soft-deleted owners.\n\n**Domain model** \u2014 `Owner.java`: added a persisted `boolean deleted` column (defaults `false` for a newly built owner) with `isDeleted()`/`setDeleted(...)`.\n\n**Delete pipeline** \u2014 `DeleteOwner.java`: instead of `repository.delete(owner)`, it now sets `deleted = true` and saves. The DELETE YAML pipeline (load \u2192 delete \u2192 respond, still returning 204 via `RespondWithNoContent`) is unchanged, so it stays 2xx.\n\n**Read path** \u2014 `GET /api/owners/{id}` still loads by id (no filtering), so it returns the retained owner. Added a read-only `deleted` boolean to the `Owner` schema in `openapi.yml`; MapStruct auto-maps `owner.deleted \u2192 OwnerDto.deleted`, so the GET body now carries `\"deleted\": false/true`.\n\n**Create checks ignore deleted** \u2014 `RequireUniqueIdentity.java` (identity + household duplicate check) and `AssignPossibleDuplicate.java` (soft-duplicate flag) now `continue` past any owner with `isDeleted()`, so a normally-blocking duplicate is allowed when the only match was soft-deleted.\n\n**Schema/seed** \u2014 added the `deleted` column to all four `db/*/schema.sql` files (`BOOLEAN DEFAULT FALSE`, `BIT DEFAULT 0` for MySQL). The test DB (hsqldb) has `ddl-auto=none`, so the column had to be in `schema.sql`; its `data.sql` uses positional inserts, so I appended `FALSE` to the 10 seed owner rows. The h2/mysql/postgres seed files use explicit column lists, so the default covers them.\n\nThe acceptance tests `coreSoftDeleteFlagsAndRetains` and `coreDeletedOwnerIgnoredByDuplicateCheck` both pass.",
